  <article class="article">
    <span class="cat-badge">Guide &middot; Rental Cost</span>
    <h1>Yamaha Sirius Daily Rental Price in Hanoi</h1>
    
    <p>The Yamaha Sirius is the other half of the cheapest price band on the Hanoi rental list: the semi-automatic underbone that matches the Honda Wave coin for coin. At this operator the Sirius rents for a verified 150,000 VND per day, the same figure as the Wave, and for the rider who wants a machine with a workhorse reputation the daily rate is where its value shows first. A day of Sirius riding costs about the same as a day of anything else in the city that is worth doing.</p>
    <p>This guide covers the verified daily price, what the rate includes, the arithmetic that decides between paying by the day and committing to a week, and how to plan the riding costs around it. Every figure matches the published <a href="/hanoi-motorbike-rental-cost/">Hanoi motorbike rental cost</a> hub, the wider machine choices sit in the <a href="/hanoi-motorbike-rental/">Hanoi motorbike rental</a> cluster, and deposit and paperwork questions are answered in the site <a href="/faq/">FAQ</a>.</p>

    <nav class="toc"><ol><li><a href="#sec-1">What the daily rate covers</a></li><li><a href="#sec-2">The verified Yamaha Sirius daily price</a></li><li><a href="#sec-3">The arithmetic: daily against weekly</a></li><li><a href="#sec-4">How the daily price compares across the fleet</a></li><li><a href="#sec-5">Planning a day of riding costs</a></li><li><a href="#sec-6">When a daily rental is the right period</a></li><li><a href="#sec-7">Moving from daily to a longer rate</a></li><li><a href="#sec-8">A counter checklist for daily renters</a></li></ol></nav>

    <section id="sec-1">
      <h2 id="sec-1">What the daily rate covers</h2>
      <p>The daily rate covers the Sirius itself for the rental day, together with what every rate at this operator includes: the machine, a helmet, and the support of the yard across the rental. It is a complete package for one day of riding, and the price is the same whether the day is a commute or a circuit of the city.</p>
      <p>What the rate does not cover is everything the city adds. Petrol goes in the tank at your own cost, parking in the busier districts is charged by the session, and any traffic fine is the rider's own affair. None of these extras are hidden; the price list states what the rental includes, and the counter repeats it at handover.</p>
      <p>The daily rate also carries no commitment beyond its day. There is nothing to extend, nothing to cancel, and nothing to return to at the end of a longer plan. For the rider whose need is measured in hours and single days, that simplicity is part of what the 150,000 VND buys. For the rider whose need is a single long day, an airport run or a moving day or a loop of the day-trip ring road, that flat single figure is the point.</p>
    </section>

    <section id="sec-2">
      <h2 id="sec-2">The verified Yamaha Sirius daily price</h2>
      <p>The published price for a daily Yamaha Sirius rental at this operator is 150,000 VND per day. Unlike the monthly band, the daily rate is a single figure, the same for every day of the week and every week of the year on the published list. The counter confirms it at handover, and the rental agreement records it.</p>
      <p>The figure sits in the lowest daily band the operator offers. The Honda Wave, Yamaha Mio and Honda Click share it exactly, which makes the choice between those four machines a matter of preference rather than price. The published list is short, and the Sirius sits in its lowest row: the cheapest complete daily rental the operator offers, next to its three stablemates.</p>
      <p>The Honda Vision, Honda Air Blade and the electric motorbike rent at 200,000 VND per day, one tier up. For the Sirius specifically, the daily rate is the entry point to a machine with a long working history in Vietnam. It shares the Wave's reputation for surviving anything a city can do to it, and it shares the price as well. Renters who want the underbone experience without paying a premium for it start here.</p>
    </section>

    <section id="sec-3">
      <h2 id="sec-3">The arithmetic: daily against weekly</h2>
      <p>The daily rate rewards the short stay. One day is 150,000 VND, two are 300,000 VND, three are 450,000 VND, four are 600,000 VND, and all four totals sit under the 700,000 VND weekly figure for the same machine. Up to four riding days, paying daily is unambiguously cheaper.</p>
      <p>The fifth day is where the arithmetic turns. Five days at 150,000 VND total 750,000 VND, which is 50,000 VND more than the weekly rate that covers the same five days and two more besides. Every day past the fourth widens the gap, and by the seventh day the daily payer has spent 1,050,000 VND against the weekly payer's 700,000 VND.</p>
      <p>The decision rule is therefore one line: four riding days or fewer, rent daily; five or more, rent weekly. The rule needs no knowledge of the yard, no negotiation, and no special terms. It is simply the published price list compared with itself, which is the cheapest research a renter will ever do. Doing it at the counter takes a sentence; doing it at the end of the rental takes an apology.</p>
    </section>

    <section id="sec-4">
      <h2 id="sec-4">How the daily price compares across the fleet</h2>
      <p>The 150,000 VND band holds four machines: the Honda Wave, the Yamaha Sirius, the Yamaha Mio and the Honda Click. The Wave and Sirius are semi-automatic underbones with foot gears, while the Mio and Click are fully automatic scooters, so the band spans both riding styles at one price. That is the most useful fact on the price list for a first-time renter.</p>
      <p>One tier up, the 200,000 VND daily band holds the Honda Vision, the Honda Air Blade and the electric motorbike. The Vision is the premium automatic scooter, the Air Blade the larger semi-automatic, and the electric machine the quiet option. Renters moving up pay a third more per day for those differences.</p>
      <p>The Sirius's own place in the structure is plain: it is the cheapest way to rent a semi-automatic underbone, tied with the Wave, and it undercuts everything in the upper band by 50,000 VND a day. Over a four-day stay that difference is 200,000 VND, real money for a price comparison that takes a minute. The Honda Wave's equivalent daily math is laid out in the <a href="/hanoi-motorbike-rental-cost/honda-wave-daily-price/">Honda Wave daily price</a> guide. Renters who plan to compare both underbones over several days will find the same arithmetic on both pages, which is itself the answer: the price will not decide, so the seat must.</p>
    </section>

    <section id="sec-5">
      <h2 id="sec-5">Planning a day of riding costs</h2>
      <p>Fuel is the first cost on top of the rate, and the Sirius keeps it small. The machine is built for economy, and a day of city riding uses a modest amount of petrol whichever districts the day includes. Ask at handover what fuel level is expected at return; the answer is usually a level agreement, level in and level out. The level agreement matters more on a single day than on any longer rental, because there is no averaging across days to hide a difference.</p>
      <p>Parking is the second cost, and it depends entirely on where the day goes. Street parking in the busier districts is charged by the session, and a day that touches the Old Quarter several times can meet the charge several times. Ask the counter which areas charge and which side streets are free; the answer is local knowledge worth more than the minute it takes to ask.</p>
      <p>The third cost is the fine to avoid rather than pay: the city's traffic rules apply to a rented Sirius exactly as they do to an owned one. A day that ends without a fine is a day at the published price; a day that ends with one is not. None of these costs reach the rental rate itself, which is why the 150,000 VND figure stays the anchor of the daily budget.</p>
    </section>

    <section id="sec-6">
      <h2 id="sec-6">When a daily rental is the right period</h2>
      <p>The daily rate fits three situations. The first is the short visit: the traveller with two or three riding days inside a longer stay, who pays only for the days actually ridden. The second is the errand: the resident whose own machine is in a shop, who needs wheels for a day or two and no longer.</p>
      <p>The third is the trial. The Sirius has its fans and its skeptics, and the daily rate is the cheapest way to form an opinion before committing to anything longer. A trial day also settles the gear question: the Sirius shifts with a foot lever and no clutch, and an hour of city traffic tells a renter more than any description can. A renter who likes the machine can move up to the weekly or monthly rate with the yard; a renter who does not has spent one day's price to find out. Renters planning a longer term will find the options gathered in the <a href="/monthly-motorbike-rental-hanoi/">monthly motorbike rental Hanoi</a> hub.</p>
      <p>What all three situations share is honest arithmetic: the daily rate is right exactly when the riding days are few and known. The moment the day count creeps past four, the rate stops being the cheapest option, and the next section is the conversation to have instead.</p>
    </section>

    <section id="sec-7">
      <h2 id="sec-7">Moving from daily to a longer rate</h2>
      <p>The Sirius price list makes the move easy to see. The weekly rate is 700,000 VND and the monthly band is 900,000 to 1,200,000 VND, both published alongside the daily figure. A renter whose plans grow can ask the counter to move the rental onto the longer period rather than keep stacking days.</p>
      <p>The best time to ask is early. A switch agreed on day three of a growing stay saves money from that day forward; a switch agreed on day six has already paid the daily premium for two days too many. The counter handles these conversations routinely, and the rental agreement is where the new period, rate and dates are written.</p>
      <p>The move is also where the Sirius's price band matters most: because the weekly and monthly figures are the same as the Wave's, lengthening the rental never forces a machine change. The renter who likes the Sirius keeps the Sirius, and the arithmetic stays the arithmetic.</p>
    </section>

    <section id="sec-8">
      <h2 id="sec-8">A counter checklist for daily renters</h2>
      <p>Six questions make a day safe. The rate, 150,000 VND, confirmed on the agreement. The dates, which day and what time the day ends. The deposit terms, amount and conditions for return, in words you can repeat. The fuel arrangement, level in and level out.</p>
      <p>The fifth question is the support channel: who to call if the machine needs attention mid-day. The sixth is the upgrade question: what the weekly rate costs and how to switch if the stay grows. Six answers, five minutes, and a day that behaves from the first key turn.</p>
      <p>Motorbike Rental - Nguyen Tu, 112 Nguyen Van Cu, Long Bien, Hanoi, open daily from 09:00 to 21:00, rents the Yamaha Sirius by the day at the verified rate of 150,000 VND. Bring the six questions, and the day costs exactly what the price list says it costs.</p>
    </section>
    <section id="faq">
      <h2>Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
      <article class="faq-entry"><h2>How much does it cost to rent a Yamaha Sirius for a day in Hanoi?</h2><p>At this operator the verified daily rate is 150,000 VND for the Yamaha Sirius. The counter confirms the figure at handover and it is recorded on the rental agreement.</p></article>
      <article class="faq-entry"><h2>Is the Yamaha Sirius cheaper to rent than the Honda Wave?</h2><p>No. Both machines share the same verified price band: 150,000 VND per day, 700,000 VND per week and 900,000 to 1,200,000 VND per month. The choice between them is about the machine, not the price.</p></article>
      <article class="faq-entry"><h2>When is the weekly rate cheaper than paying daily for a Sirius?</h2><p>From the fifth riding day. Five days at 150,000 VND total 750,000 VND, more than the 700,000 VND weekly rate, so any stay with five or more riding days is cheaper weekly.</p></article>
      <article class="faq-entry"><h2>Does the daily price include fuel and parking?</h2><p>No. The rate covers the machine, a helmet and the support of the yard. Fuel and parking are riding costs on top, and the yard can advise which districts charge for street parking.</p></article>
      <article class="faq-entry"><h2>How does the Sirius daily price compare with other models?</h2><p>The Sirius shares its 150,000 VND daily band with the Honda Wave, Yamaha Mio and Honda Click. The Honda Vision, Honda Air Blade and electric motorbike rent at 200,000 VND per day.</p></article>
      <article class="faq-entry"><h2>Where can I rent a Yamaha Sirius by the day in Hanoi?</h2><p>Motorbike Rental - Nguyen Tu, 112 Nguyen Van Cu, Long Bien, Hanoi, open daily from 09:00 to 21:00, rents the Yamaha Sirius by the day at the verified rate of 150,000 VND.</p></article>
    </section>

